@node emacsctl Internals
@section emacsctl Internals

@subsection Instance resolution

@file{ctl-transport-dbus.c} resolves the @option{--instance}
selector to a bus name: explicit PID ->
@code{org.cmacs.Editor.Pid<PID>}; @code{primary} -> the well-known
@code{org.cmacs.Editor}; @code{auto}/unset -> @env{CMACS_DBUS_NAME}
if set, else the single @code{Pid*} name if exactly one instance
exists, else the @emph{newest} instance --- most recently started
process, compared by @file{/proc/<pid>/stat} start time with a
highest-PID fallback on hosts without procfs; no instances at all is
exit 4.  Enumeration uses @code{ListNames} only and every call
passes @code{G_DBUS_CALL_FLAGS_NO_AUTO_START}, so emacsctl can never
D-Bus-activate a stray editor.

@subsection Exit-code mapping

@file{ctl-error.c}: the @code{CTL_ERROR} domain carries
usage/unsupported/no-instance directly;
@code{G_DBUS_ERROR_UNKNOWN_INTERFACE} / @code{UNKNOWN_METHOD} -> 3;
@code{SERVICE_UNKNOWN} / @code{NAME_HAS_NO_OWNER} -> 4; everything
else -> 1.  The ssh transport re-materializes remote D-Bus error
names across the tunnel so the same mapping applies remotely.

@subsection The frame codec

@file{ctl-frame.c}: 4-byte big-endian length + one JSON object,
capped at 64 MiB.  Variants are embedded as two members ---
@code{KEY} (JSON) and @code{KEY_sig} (the GVariant type string that
directs deserialization).  Truncation and malformed payloads error;
clean EOF reports via an out parameter; all paths are unit-tested in
@file{cmacs/emacsctl/tests/test-ctl.c}.

@subsection Watch plumbing

@code{CtlWatcher} subscribes through the @emph{transport interface},
so @code{logs -f} / @code{watch stream} are identical locally and
over ssh.  Each incoming D-Bus signal becomes an @code{event}
GObject-signal carrying a @code{CtlResult}, rendered through the
active formatter --- @option{-o json} log streaming works.  On the
server side, @code{MessageLogged} is driven by a 500 ms GLib tick in
@file{cmacs/dbus/cmacs-dbus-iface-log.c} that diffs
@file{*Messages*} by position, catching both C- and Lisp-originated
messages with zero upstream-file edits.

@subsection Memory and threading

Single-threaded throughout.  All Lisp evaluation happens in the
@emph{editor's} process via its safe dispatch layer
(@file{cmacs/glib/cmacs-eval-dispatch.c}) --- emacsctl itself never
embeds a Lisp.
